The tests of this account
user-29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
A test is a BASIC program whose record id starts with test. (test.sort, test.invoice.totals) in one of your program files: bp, a file whose name ends .bp, or one with a file of the same name and .out beside it. It prints what it checks, and the LAST non-empty line it prints says how it went: 3 passed, 0 failed (a name and a colon may come first, as in sandbox-x: 12 passed, 0 failed). The page judges that line: PASS when failed is 0 and passed is above 0; FAIL when failed is above 0 or the run aborted; NO VERDICT for anything else (no such line, nothing printed, or 0 passed and 0 failed).
R runs the test under the cursor, A runs every test shown (the filter applies) and F runs the ones that failed again, one after another, each after a box that gives the number of tests and the first commands (Cancel is what Enter chooses); Esc stops after the test that is running. Each is run bp test.name with the output collected. It needs no gate and no access to the operating system, only the object code of your own program. A test with no object code cannot be run: B compiles it (basic bp test.name) after a box that shows the command, and compiling needs the bp-edit gate. Enter (or F6) moves to the Output box and shows what the test printed, scrolling, and Esc (or Shift-F6) comes back to the list. The results are kept for the session only and written nowhere.
A test that asks for input is given empty answers, and the result says how many it used. BASIC has no time limit and neither has the page: a test that does not end is stopped with Ctrl-C and then Q at the prompt that appears. Only the first and the last lines of a very long output are kept; the verdict is judged on all of it. A name with a capital, an ampersand or a dollar is listed but not run from here: the page tells you what to type.
